# ClickHouse — events, sessions and rollups.
#
# Read by `clickhouse/oa-entrypoint.sh`, which renders the four least-privilege
# users into a `users.d` drop-in at every container start. NOTHING ELSE CREATES
# THEM: there is no first-boot SQL and the migration runner does not do it
# either, whatever an older `.env.example` may have claimed.
#
#   oa_ingest       INSERT on the tables the worker writes, plus the SELECTs
#                   ClickHouse requires while pushing to materialized views
#   oa_read         SELECT on analytics.* — the query gateway, and only it
#   oa_maintenance  ALTER DELETE — site and account deletion, worker only
#   oa_migration    DDL — the migration runner, and nothing long-running
#
# Each password is repeated in the env file of the ONE service entitled to it.
# `./generate-secrets.sh` keeps them in step.
#
# Changing any of these needs a RECREATE, not a restart: `docker compose
# restart` re-runs the entrypoint with the container's ORIGINAL environment, so
# the new value silently never arrives. Use
# `docker compose up -d --force-recreate clickhouse`.
CLICKHOUSE_INGEST_PASSWORD={{CLICKHOUSE_INGEST_PASSWORD}}
CLICKHOUSE_READ_PASSWORD={{CLICKHOUSE_READ_PASSWORD}}
CLICKHOUSE_MAINTENANCE_PASSWORD={{CLICKHOUSE_MAINTENANCE_PASSWORD}}
CLICKHOUSE_MIGRATION_PASSWORD={{CLICKHOUSE_MIGRATION_PASSWORD}}

# Which addresses the four users above may connect from. The default trusts the
# compose network, which is the only thing that can reach this container while
# it publishes no ports. Narrow it if you move ClickHouse onto a host others can
# route to — and bind a private address in config.d/openanalytics.xml too.
# OA_CLICKHOUSE_ALLOWED_NETWORK=0.0.0.0/0
